<article class="env-remark" id="063Z">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063Z">Remark <span data-tag="063Z">9.3.1.15</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-cocomplete: that is, it admits $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered colimits. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The tautological map $H: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ admits a left adjoint $F: \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Moreover, if these conditions are satisfied, then the functor $F$ is a $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }$-extension of the identity functor $\operatorname{id}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ (more precisely, if $\alpha : \operatorname{id}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \rightarrow H \circ F$ is the unit of an adjunction, then $\alpha $ exhibits $F$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }$-extension of $\operatorname{id}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$). See Proposition <a data-tag="0600" href="/tag/0600">8.4.6.13</a>. </p>
</article>
